hey guys I'm Alex welcome back to my  apartment so last night I was hanging  out sitting on the couch watching some  Netflix might involve some Tigers and I  bought it with these strongest sweet  tooth but this time I was out of luck I  don't have anything to eat so I got a  little creative and I came up with  something that was so delicious that I  am so excited to share with all of you  this is a recipe for my brown butter  pantry cookies I have own pantry cookies  because you can literally throw anything  you have in your pantry in this what's  also really cool about this cookie is  that it doesn't use any flour at all  alright enough talking let's get cooking  alright here we are back in my kitchen  now like I said this is a totally  customizable cookie that's why I call  them pantry cookies for me I'm gonna be  using some coconut some chocolate chips  and some chopped walnuts if you don't  have these ingredients on hand don't  stress you could switch out walnuts for  pecans you could add some dried fruit  you can just totally omit the coconut  whatever works for you for the flower  we're gonna be using oats these are  whole rolled oats I'm gonna be putting  these in the blender to grind it up into  an oat flour we have two sources of  sweetness here we've got brown sugar and  maple syrup they just work really well  together if you don't have maple syrup  you can swap and hunting or you could  bump up the brown sugar lastly just some  other basic pantry items baking soda  salt butter and that's it all right time  to make the dough  I know flour can be super hard to come  by these days  so I really wanted to create a delicious  recipe so that it didn't stop you from  baking making a flower is super easy I'm  just gonna pop these rules oats into my  blender and blend  all right and just like that we've got a  flower so do you take a look it's  actually very similar to the consistency  of regular flour it's a little bit  grittier of course because we're using  oats and grinding ourselves so now that  our oat flour is done or giving a move  in on the rest of our dry ingredients so  to the oat flour I'm gonna be adding a  teaspoon of salt I'm also gonna add a  half teaspoon of baking soda and finally  a half a cup of full rolled oats I like  using full rolled oats on top of the oak  lower because it just gives that added  texture to the final cookie you want to  make sure it's fully mixed and we're  gonna set our dry ingredients aside for  now all right moving on to probably my  favorite part about this cookie the  brown butter gives this awesome  luxurious rich nuttiness to your cookie  but don't let that intimidate you it's  super easy to make melt your butter down  and get those brown bits and thank me  later it's also helpful to use a lighter  pan so that you can check the color of  the butter as it cooks but it's not  necessary I don't have a lighter pan so  this is what I'm using or maybe you're  going to want to pay close attention to  your butter because there's only a few  seconds between brown butter and Burke  butter and we don't want burnt butter in  this recipe all right 6 tablespoons of  butter going in you want to keep a close  watch of your butter once it's melted  you're gonna want to keep stirring we  just want to make sure it doesn't burn  again making brown butter takes a few  extra minutes here but it's worth it I  promise all right so now my butter has  completely melted down and I'm gonna  keep stirring you'll notice that your  butter starts to foam up that's totally  normal and then eventually you'll notice  some brown bits forming at the bottom  keep it stirring and once it's brown  we'll take it off the heat our butter is  brown it looks good smells super  delicious  all right into our brown butter I'm  gonna add 1/3 of a cup of brown sugar  fourth of a cup of maple syrup again a  super awesome flavor from the maple  syrup here one egg  and now whisk you're gonna want to mix  this up pretty well I always try to do  that having mixing well everything is  separated and then once we add in the  dry ingredients you're gonna want to  switch to more of like a full  alright the wet is mixed so now I'm  gonna start adding our dry ingredients  to the wet ingredients and again I'm  switching to a spatula to fold it all  together the dough is gonna look fairly  wet when it comes together don't worry  as the dough sits the oats will continue  to absorb some of that moisture oh it  smells so good alright and finally I'm  gonna add in the yummy stuff so again  I'm using shredded unsweetened coconut  chopped walnuts and chocolate chips you  can swap in whatever you have on hand  don't stress I'm adding a half a cup of  each of these toppings just cuz this  makes for a really nice chunky textured  cookie give it a final mix alright so  now I'm gonna let this dough hang out on  the counter at room temperature for  about ten to fifteen minutes this will  help the dough absorb some of that  moisture so that it comes together more  easily you could also pop it in the  fridge but that will result in like a  fluffier cookie and I like my cookies a  little bit flatter and chillier I also  should mention that I have my oven  preheating to 350 degrees alright see  you soon our dough has been hanging out  for about 15 minutes  if you give it a little mix you'll  notice that it's a lot thicker than it  was originally so that's a really  important step so I just use about a  spoonful of dough then I transfer it  into my hand  and roll it into a ball and then just  pop it on the sheet the cookies don't  spread out too much so you don't have to  worry about them being too close but I  still leave like an inch apart the dough  is a little bit sticky so your hands  will probably get a little messy but it  just adds to the fun this is definitely  an activity you can do with kids working  for the cookies I'm sure they won't mind  last one for this tray beautiful no not  saying I've tried this dough because  there is egg in there but it's really  good I'm gonna use a spoon it to flatten  these cookies out this will help you get  the perfect cookie shape if your spoon  sticks too much too  the dough you can oil the back of it so  that it comes off nice and easy and I  just try to go around with my finger and  kind of reshape them just to make sure  they're nice and round finally and this  is totally optional I'm gonna add a  little bit of flaky salt on the top of  each one I really like flaky salt on  cookies just adds a little like  something some alright and now we bake  so I'm gonna make these at 350 degrees  for about seven minutes they cook pretty  quickly which is the plus just check  them to make sure they don't burn  alright our timer just went off I'm  gonna check on the cookies those are  looking so good  so these cookies are a little bit  fragile because we're using oak flour so  I'm gonna let them cool a bit on the pan  itself because if I try to transfer them  now they'll probably break then I'll  transfer them to a plate to cool even  more and then we've got our cookies and  there you have it our brown butter  pantry cookies are done they smell so  good  I cannot wait to try it mmm these are so  good they've got the perfect balance of  salty to sweet they've got so much  texture from all the stuff we put in  there the caramelized sweetness from the  brown sugar and the maple syrup goes so  well with that brown butter and the  walnuts mmm  definitely a cookie that will satisfy  the sweet tooth my sweet tooth  specifically all right well I hope you  enjoyed this video if you make these  cookies please tag me I'd love to see it  and remember stay home wash your hands  and eat a cookie oh yes  [Music]
 

